 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-4I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c8360c-3a50-5dde-b1bc-982c3982cc9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404282} {:answer/id #uuid "f6699882-8461-5d24-9c25-bf00717bab1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404283} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404284} {:answer/id #uuid "77ed7e08-d0f9-52c5-bb87-4330aef31eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404285} {:answer/id #uuid "4129fe51-c35a-5376-82e4-222db49da083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404284} :answer-field/id #uuid "e147ba3d-c970-55a9-ac96-8187378e9cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404281}] :question/id #uuid "303c15f5-3e83-55c0-a519-b60eaa51efba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83854" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c721ea-d96e-5bf0-bc9c-8798a2a4ddf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404289} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82210a-e608-50a1-bf42-9e7411d614b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404290} {:answer/id #uuid "94c87bfa-134c-55bd-87ae-8cfa1aedb050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404291} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e1ec20-80aa-55b5-9af6-1c4b410f8f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404292} {:answer/id #uuid "606da9ae-0a33-5676-904e-bbed5b045212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404289} :answer-field/id #uuid "99303798-a587-5408-b480-605526c75a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404288}] :question/id #uuid "497d051f-d1fb-59f1-b865-99c679ab4f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-83860" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd25d24-ca14-582c-9c9a-901bf68be589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458873} {:answer/id #uuid "999a115a-08e8-5499-8e87-7c47414d8221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458895} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7091e-e210-5867-884f-ccfde76bb619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458923} {:answer/id #uuid "aabbbcda-6626-588e-9857-4b315ea6b330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458935} {:answer/id #uuid "e52bd489-6e03-5706-aa17-622a610be235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458873} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a726a9-a1c8-58dd-882f-1cff70681cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459106}] :question/id #uuid "800b324e-1ca8-5722-9959-a0bda670cec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83847" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18548cc4-5579-5860-af3c-2ffb7473c802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -28 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 12\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458669} {:answer/id #uuid "5d535dfb-87df-5251-bb7b-1b059f15fcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 12\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458783} {:answer/id #uuid "72fd09c1-79de-56af-8b54-d3b0e35dbc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458829} {:answer/id #uuid "87bb53d7-fc61-5729-a383-3a8cc7ab4af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458867} {:answer/id #uuid "900bcbf4-f190-5da3-839e-ba5e69558818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458829} :answer-field/id #uuid "e015e886-af0c-50c8-80c7-dbd3c9879cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459186}] :question/id #uuid "c742c85e-0648-5cd9-8c31-bb35257f03e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-84609" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following. \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector  ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector  ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ -28 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1970 :topic/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125392} :activity/id #uuid "7fb60c61-c60a-5530-b6e6-eb3f36962ebb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125781 :tutorial/content "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{S}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices." :tutorial/id #uuid "439421f9-bf09-5277-910e-bc279b222542" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3527 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "47152a53-794a-571b-bae6-bbcab9ffc719" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e73b8bcf-79c2-5326-b396-d7d3e06badaa"} :step/id #uuid "847da92e-200b-512e-810c-f5ebd91868d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124513 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2776554-94ce-5c25-b838-16fdfceddb1c"} :step/id #uuid "31c70a58-59a2-50d2-8480-d66f44d5febf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124514 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "656da168-af12-53c2-8add-2c82cf71b31c"} :step/id #uuid "3bc9c38e-6437-5798-899c-8b68da925ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124515 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd0537f8-b091-580a-974e-8e928d54939d"} :step/id #uuid "75f08151-8784-5638-af8e-e497797513e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125394}}] :activity/title "The Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124744}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124512 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124321 :question/id #uuid "6aed23e0-cd3e-5940-a722-63550bcefc97" :question/math-academy-id "e-5431" :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $\\mathb