n{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider a general vector $\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.$ Multiplying by the matrix $T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\,T\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Linear Transformation Given its Standard Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124515 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124324 :question/id #uuid "9c8d9615-6a5d-5b6a-8735-b9b2f58331f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5434" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$\n\n![](images/8d/8ddf6015972344b2afaccf34a63e5ae8c478f326a2b227c45499a1be168ce996.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})=T\\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd0537f8-b091-580a-974e-8e928d54939d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c453b655-cb33-566e-b4b9-35a21a8e2492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123261} {:answer/id #uuid "ef171c62-4fb7-57fb-aa7d-aab0d1323bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17